// ============================================================================
//  charactersDB2 — the IMPORTED character database
// ============================================================================
//
//  Same format as charactersDB.js, and deliberately a separate file.
//
//  charactersDB.js is hand-written and is the HIGHER AUTHORITY. This one is
//  where a bulk import lands — animadex first, whatever comes after it later.
//  When both files describe the same character, charactersDB wins outright and
//  the entry here is skipped; `cleanupCharacterArray` reports how many were
//  shadowed so an import that collides heavily is visible rather than silent.
//
//  Why separate rather than appended:
//
//    - An import could be a hundred entries or a hundred thousand. Keeping it
//      apart means it can be regenerated, re-sorted, diffed or deleted whole
//      without touching a line anybody wrote by hand.
//    - A hand-written entry carries decisions — which outfit is `default`, what
//      `penis` should expand to for her — that a scraped one cannot. Losing
//      those to an import that happened to run later would be unrecoverable.
//    - It gives the linter something to check an import against BEFORE it is
//      trusted: `node scripts/webui/webui2-lint.js` reads both files.
//
//  Format, in one example — see charactersDB.js for the real thing:
//
//      .codename; canonical booru tag, trait, trait, trait
//      penis; what `penis` should become for her
//      *default; the outfit tags
//
//  A line ending in a bare `;` with nothing after it is a STUB, not an entry.
//  The parser splits on "; " and a stub silently produces an unreachable
//  outfit — charactersDB.js has 25 of them today. The linter flags them; do not
//  let an import add more.
//
//  EMPTY ON PURPOSE. The animadex import is on hold pending a way to pull their
//  text database without the image payload.
// ============================================================================

var characterArray2 = `
`;
